 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes—if by KL you mean Kuala Lumpur, stay indoors as much as practical today, Saturday, October 17.** A Kuala Lumpur monitoring station reported a Malaysian Air Pollutant Index (API) of **161 (“Unhealthy”) at 7:00 a.m. local time**, with fine particles driving the reading. People with asthma are particularly vulnerable to haze, and Malaysia’s health ministry advises staying indoors where possible and reducing outdoor exercise. Air quality can change, so check the latest reading for your neighbourhood before going out. ([jerebumalaysia.com](https://jerebumalaysia.com/en/stations/cheras-w-p-kuala-lumpur/))\n\nIf you must go out, keep the trip short and avoid exertion. Indoors, keep windows closed if haze is coming in, and follow your asthma action plan with your reliever inhaler available. **If you’re struggling to breathe or your symptoms don’t improve after using your prescribed reliever, seek urgent medical care.** ([moh.gov.my](https://www.moh.gov.my/teras/info-kesihatan/jerebu/artikel-dan-panduan-pengguna-berkaitan-jerebu/panduan-kesihatan-untuk-anda?utm_source=openai))",
